    // what makes an L corner close up: the perpendicular piece's post lands on the same
    // boundary the straight run's did. The skirt below zero is buried — on a slope it
    // stops daylight showing under the piece.
    // --- Panels are a hub with four arms ----------------------------------------
    // A thin panel centred in its cell cannot turn a corner: two perpendicular
    // neighbours each run down the middle of their own cell and their centrelines
    // never meet, which is why an L came out as two walls with a notch bitten out of
    // the join. So a panel is not a slab any more. It is a post at the cell CENTRE
    // with up to four arms reaching out to the cell edges, and only the arms facing a
    // neighbour are shown. Two panels side by side each grow an arm toward the other
    // and they meet exactly on the shared edge; a corner grows two arms at right
